<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhi Dun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">314–366</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Zhi Daolin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhi Dun was a Buddhist monk and scholar of the Eastern Jin dynasty who moved in the aristocratic circles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> and the south-east. He interpreted Buddhist ideas such as emptiness in the language of Daoist 'dark learning' and was admired for his skill in 'pure conversation', as stories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishuo-xinyu">Shishuo Xinyu</a> record. His commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a> was influential, and he helped make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> respectable among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhi-dun.jpg" alt="Zhi Dun" width="307" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ren Yi (1840–96) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Monk_Zhidun_Admiring_a_Horse,_by_Ren_Yi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
